have eigenvalue $\\lambda$ because eigenvectors for $\\lambda$ might not lie in $D$. But the interval $(\\lambda-1, \\lambda)$ might intersect options? Possibly.\n\nLet\'s try a specific numeric example. Let $D = \\{ (x,0) : x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}$. We want $A|_\\mathbb{E}$ to have spectral radius $r$, i.e., the dominated/limit supremum over absolute values of eigenvalues of $A|_\\mathbb{E}$ is $r$. We want $A$ to have an eigenvalue with absolute value > $r$. But if $A$ has an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ with $|\\lambda| > r$, then consider the action on $D$: For any eigenvector $v \\in D$, $Av = \\lambda v$, and $\\lambda v \\in D$? Actually, if $v \\in D$, then $Av \\in D$? Since $A$ maps $D$ into $D$, $\\lambda v \\in D$ if $\\lambda$ is a scalar. So $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $A|_\\mathbb{E}$ as well, because if $v \\in D$, then $(A-\\lambda I)v=0$. So actually, if $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ with $|\\lambda| > r$, then it is also an eigenvalue of $A|_\\mathbb{E}$ provided there exists an eigenvector in $D$. But eigenvalues might not come from eigenvectors in $D$: sometimes an eigenvalue may only come from eigenvectors outside $D$. However, if $|\\lambda|$ is larger than the spectral radius of $A|_\\mathbb{E}$, then $\\lambda$ might not be an eigenvalue of $A|_\\mathbb{E}$ because there might be no eigenvector in $D$. But can $\\lambda$ still be an eigenvalue of $A$ even if there is no eigenvector in $D$? Yes, in the presence of Jordan blocks. For example, consider $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Eigenvalues: only 0. Eigenvector for 0: $(1,0)$. So $A|_\\mathbb{E}$ has eigenvalue 0. So spectral radius 0. That doesn\'t give a $\\lambda > 0$.\n\nTo get a larger spectral radius, we need to have eigenvalues that are not eigenvectors in $D$. Consider $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$. Eigenvalues: 1. Eigenoints: $(1,0)$. So $A|_\\mathbb{E}$ has eigenvalue 1, spectral radius 1. Full matrix has eigenvalue 1. So equal.\n\nMaybe consider a matrix with complex eigenvalues. Let $X = \\mathbb{R}^2$, $E = \\mathbb{R}$. Take $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. Eigenvalues of full matrix: solve $\\det\\begin{pmatrix} 1-\\lambda & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{pmatrix}= (1-\\lambda)(3-\\lambda)=0$, so 1 and 3. Spectral radius 3. Restriction to first coordinate: $A|_\\mathbb{E}$ is defined by $(A|_D)(x,0) = A(x,0) = 1*(x+0) = x$. So eigenvalues of $A|_D$: 1 (only one eigenvalue because it\'s a scalar multiplication? Actually, since it\'s just a linear map on $\\mathbb{R}$, it is just multiplication by 1? Wait: $A(x,0) = x$, it\'s just the identity on $\\mathbb{R}$. So eigenvalues: 1. Spectral radius 1. So here $r_E=1$, $r_A=3$. That\'s a counterexample! But we must check conditions: $A$ maps $E$ into itself: if $y \\in E$, then $y = (x,0)$, and $A(y) = (x,0) = y$, so yes, $A$ maps $E$ into $E$. $E$ is dense in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. The spectral radius of $A|_E$ is 1. The spectral radius of $A$ is 3 (since the eigenvalues are 1 and 3, the largest modulus is 3). So the answer appears to be: No, the spectral radius of $A$ on $X$ is not necessarily equal to that of the restriction if the restriction has spectral radius $r > 0$. But wait, the spectral radius of $A|_E$ is the supremum of the moduli of eigenvalues of $A|_E$. In this example, $A|_E$ is just the projection onto the first coordinate, which is the identity, so its spectral radius is 1. On $X$, $A$ has eigenvalues 1 and 3, so spectral radius is 3. So indeed $r_A \\ge 3$ and $r_E = 1$. So the spectral radius of $A$ on $X$ can be larger than that of the restriction.\n\nBut is there any constraint from density? Here density of $E$ didn\'t impose any extra condition besides $E$ being dense in $X$. So this seems to be a valid counterexample.\n\nHowever, note that in the example, $A|_\\mathbb{E}$ has only one eigenvalue (1), but the spectral radius is 1. So $r_E = 1$. The full operator has eigenvalues 1 and 3, 