nd p and q are integers. Also a and b are roots of x^2 - s x + p=0, so they are integers if s^2 - 4p is a perfect square, similarly for c,d.\n\nSo we have two equations in integers s,t,p,q. Also, s,p,t,q are such that a,b are integers (so discriminant D_a = s^2-4p is a perfect square) and c,d are integers (D_c = t^2-4q is a perfect square).\n\nWe can search over small s,t,p,q.\n\nFrom s/p + t/q = 1/2, multiply by 2pq: 2sq + 2tp = pq.\nFrom 2(s+t) = p+q+st+1, or p+q = 2(s+t) - st - 1.\n\nSubstitute p+q from the second into the first? Not straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, from s/p + t/q = 1/2, we can write q = 2tp/(p-2s)? Because: s/p = 1/2 - t/q => t/q = 1/2 - s/p, so q = t/(1/2 - s/p) = tp/(p-2s). So q = tp/(p-2s). Also, from 2(s+t) = p+q+st+1, substitute q: 2(s+t) = p + tp/(p-2s) + st + 1. Multiply by (p-2s): 2(s+t)(p-2s) = p(p-2s) + tp + (st+1)(p-2s). This is quadratic in p and s,t.\n\nMaybe we can try small s,t.\n\nSince a,b,c,d are integers, s and t are sums of two integers, so they can be any integer (except possibly some constraints). p and q are products.\n\nGiven we found some solutions with small numbers, let\'s try to find them via s,t,p,q.\n\nFor (-2,1,1,-1): a=-2, b=1 -> s=-1, p=-2. c=1, d=-1 -> t=0, q=-1.\nCheck s/p + t/q = (-1)/(-2) + 0/(-1)= 1/2 + 0 = 1/2, ok.\n2(s+t)=2(-1+0)=-2, p+q+st+1 = -2-1+(-1)*0+1=-3, not equal? Wait, we need to check equation (2): 2(a+b+c+d) = ab+cd+(a+b)(c+d)+1. For this quadruple, a+b=-1, c+d=0, ab=-2, cd=-1, (a+b)(c+d)=0, so right side = -2-1+0+1=-2, left side = 2*(-1+0)=-2, ok.\nNow using s,t,p,q: left side 2(s+t)=2(-1+0)=-2, right side p+q+st+1 = -2-1+(-1)*0+1=-2, so it\'s consistent.\n\nSo (-2,1,1,-1) works with s=-1, p=-2, t=0, q=-1.\n\nFor (-1,1,4,4): a=-1,b=1 -> s=0, p=-1; c=4,d=4 -> t=8, q=16.\ns/p + t/q = 0/(-1)+8/16=0+0.5=0.5, ok.\n2(s+t)=2(0+8)=16, p+q+st+1 = -1+16+0*8+1=16, ok.\n\nFor (6,1,3,-1): a=6,b=1 -> s=7, p=6; c=3,d=-1 -> t=2, q=-3.\ns/p + t/q = 7/6 + 2/(-3)=7/6 - 2/3=7/6-4/6=3/6=1/2, ok.\n2(s+t)=2(7+2)=18, p+q+st+1 = 6-3+7*2+1=6-3+14+1=18, ok.\n\nSo we have three types.\n\nNow maybe there are more.\n\nLet\'s try to find other small solutions.\n\nWe already searched some triples (b,c,d) and found these. We should search more triples with small absolute values.\n\nWe\'ll consider b,c,d from -5 to 5, and compute a from the formula if denominator nonzero. For each triple, check if a is integer and also check equation (1) with that a. But note: When we compute a from the formula, it ensures equation (2) is satisfied. So we only need to check equation (1). So we can do:\n\nFor b,c,d in some range, compute T = b+c+d. If T=2, handle separately. Otherwise compute a = (bc+bd+cd - 2T + 1)/(2-T). Check if a is integer. Then check if 1/a+1/b+1/c+1/d = 1/2. If yes, then (a,b,c,d) is a solution. Also, we should consider that b,c,d are not necessarily the ones we fixed; any permutation of b,c,d will yield the same a, but the quadruple (a,b,c,d) will be different if we permute b,c,d because the formula assumed a is the variable solved for. Actually, if we permute b,c,d, we get the same a, but the quadruple (a,b,c,d) changes because b,c,d are in specific positions. So if we find a triple (b,c,d) that yields integer a and satisfies (1), then for each permutation of (b,c,d), we get a valid quadruple provided that in that permutation, the computed a (which is the same) satisfies (1). But note: When we permute b,c,d, we are not using a different formula; we are just reordering the triple. Since the formula for a is symmetric in b,c,d, the computed a will be the same for all permutations of the triple. So if for a triple (b,c,d) and its permutations, the computed a satisfies (1), then all permutations yield valid quadruples. However, we must also check that the resulting quadruple satisfies both equations. But since we derived a from equation (2), equation (2) is satisfied. And equation (1) is checked. So yes, each ordering of the triple gives a distinct quadruple if b,c,d are distinct. If they are not distinct, some orderings yield the same quadruple.\n\nAlso, we need to consider the case when T=2. In that case, we cannot use the formula because denominator is zero. So we solve separately for triples with b+c+d=2. We\'ll handle that later.\n\nWe\'ll search for b,c,d in a reasonable range, say from -5 to 5, and also consider T=2.\n\nLet\'s list triples (b,c,d) that yield integer a and possibly satisfy (1).\n\nWe already found:\n- (1,1,-1): T=1, a=-2.\n- (1,1,-2): T=0, a=-1.\n- (1,3,-1): T=3, a=6.\n- (1,4,4): T=9, a=-1? Actually for (1,4,4): T=9, a=(1*4+1*4+4*4 -2*9+1)/(2-9)= (4+4+16-18+1)/(-7)=7/(-7)=-1, so yes.\n- (4,1,4): same as (1,4,4).\n- (1,4,-1): T=4, a=6? Actually (1,4,-1): T=4, denominator=2-4=-2, numerator: 4-1-4=-1, a=(-1-8+1)/(-2)=(-8)/(-2)=4? Wait compute: bc=1*4=4, bd=1*(-1)=-1, cd=4*(-1)=-4, sum=4-1-4=-1, so a = (-1 -2*4+1)/(2-4)= (-1-8+1)/(-2)= (-8)/(-2)=4. So a=4. That would give quadruple (4,1,4,-1). Check (1): 1/4+1/1+1/4+1/(-1)=0.25+1+0.25-1=0.5, works. Check (2): S=4+1+4-1=8, left 16; right: ab=4*1=4, cd=4*(-1)=-4, (a+b)(c+d)=(4+1)(4-1)=5*3=15, sum=4-4+15+1=16, works. So (4,1,4,-1) is another solution. But is (1,4,-1) a triple we haven\'t considered? Yes. So we have another: (1,4,-1) gives a=4, so quadruple (4,1,4,-1). But also permutations of (b,c,d) give other quadruples.\n\n- (1,4,-4): T=1, denominator=1, numerator: 4-4-16=-16, a=(-16-2+1)/1=-17, quad: (-17,1,4,-4). Check (1): -1/17+1+1/4-1/4=-1/17+1=16/17, not 1/2.\n- (1,5,?): Let\'s try.\n\n- (2,2,?) We tried some and got no solutions.\n\n- (1,5,?):\nb=1,c=5,d=1: T=7, denominator=-5, numerator:5+1+5=11, a=(11-14+1)/(-5)=(-2)/(-5)=2/5, no.\nb=1,c=5,d=2: T=8, denominator=-6, numerator:5+2+10=17, a=(17-16+1)/(-6)=2/(-6)=-1/3, no.\nb=1,c=5,d=3: T=9, denominator=-7, numerator:5+3+15=23, a=(23-18+1)/(-7)=6/(-7)=-6/7, no.\nb=1,c=5,d=4: T=10, denominator=-8, numerator:5+4+20=29, a=(29-20+1)/(-8)=10/(-8)=-5/4, no.\nb=1,c=5,d=5: T=11, denominator=-9, numerator:5+5+25=35, a=(35-22+1)