n the supremum of f([0,1)) and the infimum of f([2,3)). As argued, if there is a discontinuity inside [0,1), then the image of [0,1) is not an interval. But is it possible that the image of [0,1) misses some values, but the image of [2,3] covers those missing values? Since values from [0,1) are all < f(2), and values from [2,3] are all ≥ f(2), the missing values in [0,1)\'s image that are less than f(2) cannot be covered by [2,3] because [2,3] gives values ≥ f(2). So if the image of [0,1) misses a value v < f(2), then v is not in f(S), so f(S) would miss that v, and if there is also a value > v in f(S), then f(S) would not be connected. Therefore, to have f(S) connected, the image of [0,1) must contain all values up to and including the infimum of f([2,3))? Actually, it must be an interval from f(0) to the infimum of f([2,3]), or at least, the union must cover all numbers between f(0) and the infimum. But if the image of [0,1) is not an interval (due to a discontinuity), it misses some numbers between the discontinuity limits, and those missing numbers are below the infimum of [2,3] (since the discontinuity limits are below f(2)). So they would be gaps. Hence, to avoid gaps, f must be continuous on [0,1). Similarly, consider any y0 in [2,3]. If there is a discontinuity at y0, then the image of [2,3] misses some values between the left-hand limit and the right-hand limit at y0, which are > f(2) but possibly less than f(3). These missing values are above f(2). Could they be covered by the image of [0,1)? The image of [0,1) is all less than f(2) (since max of f([0,1)) is at most lim_{x->1^-} f(x) and f(2) > lim_{x->1^-} f(x) if continuous on [0,1) and if f(2) > lim_{x->1^-} f(x), then they are disjoint). But if f(2) = lim_{x->1^-} f(x), then the image of [0,1) includes values up to that limit. But the discontinuity gap in [2,3) is above f(2) (since f(x0+) > f(2) if there is a discontinuity? Actually, if there is a discontinuity at y0, then f(y0-) > f(2) because y0 > 2, and by monotonicity f(y0-) > f(2). So the gap is above f(2). So it is above the supremum of f([0,1))? Actually, if f is continuous on [0,1), then the image of [0,1) is [f(0), lim_{x->1^-} f(x)] and lim_{x->1^-} f(x) = f(2) if f(2) is the infimum of [2,3]? Not exactly: lim_{x->1^-} f(x) could be less than f(2). In that case, the gap in the discontinuity of [2,3) is above f(2), which is above lim_{x->1^-} f(x). So the missing values in the discontinuity gap are above the image of [0,1) and below f(3) (maybe). But if the image of [0,1) does not contain values above lim_{x->1^-} f(x), then there is a gap between lim_{x->1^-} f(x) and f(2) (if they are not equal) and also a gap within [2,3]. To avoid gaps, we need that lim_{x->1^-} f(x) = f(2) and also that f has no discontinuities in [2,3)? If there is a discontinuity in [2,3), then the image of [2,3] misses values above f(2). But if lim_{x->1^-} f(x) = f(2), then the image of [0,1) goes up to f(2). However, f(2) is in the image of [2,3] (since 2 is in S). So the point f(2) is covered. But what about values between f(2) and the next discontinuity in [2,3)? They are not covered by [2,3] because there is a gap. Could they be covered by [0,1)? No, because [0,1) only gives values ≤ f(2). So they would be missing. Hence, f(S) would not be connected. Therefore, to avoid gaps, f must have no discontinuities in either [0,1) or [2,3]. So f is continuous on each of these intervals.\n\nBut wait, could there be a discontinuity at the boundary between the two intervals? f is defined separately on [0,1) and [2,3]. There is no point in S that connects them because there is a gap: S = [0,1) ∪ [2,3], so there is no x such that x is in both intervals. So f could potentially have discontinuities at points in [0,1) and at points in [2,3], but we argued they would create gaps. So the only possibility for f(S) to be connected is that f is continuous on both intervals, and that the images of these intervals join properly. That requires lim_{x->1^-} f(x) = f(2). But we also need that f(S) is an interval. If f is continuous on both intervals, then f(S) = (f(0), lim_{x->1^-} f(x)] ∪ [f(2), f(3)] (or with proper inclusion). For this to be an interval, we need lim_{x->1^-} f(x) = f(2). Then f(S) = [f(0), f(3)] (or with careful endpoints: if lim_{x->1^-} f(x) = f(2) and f(2) is attained, then f(2) is in both parts? Actually f(2) is only in the second part, but lim_{x->1^-} f(x) is not attained, so f(S) = [f(0), f(1-)) ∪ [f(2), f(3)] = [f(0), f(1-)] ∪ [f(2), f(3)] with f(1-) = f(2) and f(2) included, so it becomes [f(0), f(3)] if f(1-) is included, but careful: The first interval is [f(0), f(1-)] (if f is continuous on [0,1), the image is [f(0), lim_{x->1^-} f(x)] because the left-closed domain [0,1) yields an image that is left-closed (at 0) and right-open at the limit). So it is [f(0), lim f(1^-)]. For the second interval [2,3], the image is [f(2), f(3)]. Since lim f(1^-) = f(2) and f(2) is included, the union is [f(0), f(1^-)] ∪ [f(2), f(3)] = [f(0), f(3)] because f(1^-) is included from the first interval and f(2) is included from the second. So indeed it is the interval [f(0), f(3)]. Also note that f is strictly increasing, so f(0) < f(1^-) = f(2) < f(3). So f(S) is a connected interval.\n\nThus, if f is strictly increasing and f(S) is connected, then f must be continuous on S. That is, f has no discontinuities at all.\n\nBut wait: Could there be exactly two discontinuities? Possibly if f has a discontinuity at some point in [0,1) and a discontinuity at some point in [2,3)? But as argued, these discontinuities create gaps that are not filled because the discontinuity in [0,1) creates a gap below f(2) and the discontinuity in [2,3) creates a gap above f(2). So overall, the image would be disconnected. More formally, suppose f has a discontinuity at x0 in [0,1). Then there exists a gap (f(x0-), f(x0+)) in the image of [0,1). Since f(x0+) < f(2), this gap is below f(2). Also, suppose f has a discontinuity at y0 in [2,3). Then there is a gap (f(y0-), f(y0)) in the image of [2,3]. Since f(y0-) > f(2), this gap is above f(2). Then the overall image f(S) consists of three pieces: (1) values from f([0,1)) up to f(x0-); (2) the gap (f(x0-), f(x0+)) which is not attained; (3) the gap (f(y0-), f(y0)) which is not attained; (4) values from f(y0+) onward. There are gaps between (1) and (2) and between (2) and (3) and between (3) and (4) because (1) ends at f(x0-) and (2) starts at f(x0+), but f(x0+) > f(x0-), so there is a gap between f(x0-) and f(x0+) that is exactly the missing part of the gap. Actually, the gap itself is missing, but it also separates the intervals: the set (1) and (3) are disjoint because (1) contains values ≤ f(x0-) and (3) contains values ≥ f(y0-) > f(x0+). So there is a gap between f(x0-) and f(y0-). Also, within (3) there is a missing part. So f(S) is disconnected. Even if there is only one discontinuity, say in [0,1), the gap (f(x0-), f(x0+)) is missing, and it separates the two images because f(x0+) < f(2). So there is a gap between f([0,1)) and f([2,3]) if f(x0+) < f(2). If f(x0+) = f(2), then the gap in [0,1) could be filled? But if f(x0+) = f(2), then the right-hand limit at the discontinuity equals the infimum of f on [2,3]. But then the image of [0,1) includes values up to f(x0-) but not up to f(x0+)? Actually, at the discontinuity, f(x0-) < f(x0+) = f(2). So the image of [0,1) includes values ≥ f(x0-)? Actually, because f is increasing, the image of [0,1) is an interval from f(0) to the supremum, which is f(2) if f(x0+) = f(2)? But careful: f is strictly increasing, so for x < x0, f(x) < f(x0-); for x > x0, f(x) > f(x0-). But if f(x0+) = f(2), then the image includes values approaching f(2) from above (since for x > x0, f(x) > f(x0-) and increases to f(2) as x approaches x0 from the right? Actually, if there is a discontinuity at x0, then for x > x0, f(x) can increase from f(x0+) to higher values. But f(x0+) = f(2). So the image of [0,1) includes f(2)? Yes, because if x can be such that f(x) = f(2)? But if x0 is a discontinuity point, f(x0+) = f(2), but f(x0) is not defined at x0? Actually f is defined at x0. But because there is a discontinuity, f(x0) might be either less than f(2) or greater than f(2)? Actually, if f is strictly increasing and has a discontinuity at x0, it could either increase left limit then jump, meaning f(x0-) < f(x0) < f(x0+). Or it could be left sudden? But for a discontinuity in a monotone function, the left-hand limit is less than the right-hand limit. So f(x0-) < f(x0). Also, since f is increasing, f(2) could be equal to f(x0+) or greater? But since x0 < 2, f(x0+) < f(2) typically because f is increasing and 2 > x0. But if we force f(x0+) = f(2), then because x0 < 2, we have f(x0+) ≤ f(2) by monotonicity, but to have equality, we need that f is constant between x0 and 2? But f is strictly increasing, so it cannot be constant. Therefore, if x0 < 2, then f(x0+) < f(2). So it\'s impossible for the right-hand limit of a discontinuity in [0,1) to equal f(2). So the discontinuity in [0,1) always creates a gap below f(2). Therefore, f(S) cannot be connected if there is a discontinuity in [0,1). Similarly, if there is a discontinuity in [2,3), the left-hand limit f(y0-) > f(2) (since y0 > 2 and f is increasing, f(y0-) > f(2)). So the gap in [2,3) is above f(2). And f(2) is in the image of [0,1)? Actually, f(2) = f(2) is in the image. So there is a gap between f([0,1)) and f([2,3]) if f([0,1)) does not include values up to f(y0-)? But f([0,1]) max is at most lim_{x->1^-} f(x). That must be less than f(y0-) because lim_{x->1^-} f(x) < f(2) ≤ f(y0-). So there is a gap between lim_{x->1^-} f(x) and f(y0-). That gap includes f(2) if lim_{x->1^-} f(x) < f(2) ≤ f(y0-). But if lim_{x->1^-} f(x) = f(2), then the gap exists only within [2,3]. So again, there is a gap. So the only possibility for connectedness is that f has no discontinuities in either interval.\n\nThus, f must be continuous on S. But is it possible that f has one discontinuity? Possibly at a point where the jump is exactly zero? But f is strictly increasing, so jumps are positive. So no discontinuity with jump zero.\n\nTherefore, f has no discontinuities. So the correct statement is: f is continuous.\n\nDouble-check: Could f have infinitely many discontinuities? Such a function would be strictly increasing with a family of jumps. However, since f(S) is connected (an interval), the range is bounded because the domain is bounded. The total sum of jumps must be finite and actually, since the range is an interval from some minimum to some maximum, the sum of all jumps must equal the total variation, which is finite. But is it possible to construct strictly increasing functions with infinitely many discontinuities such that the overall range is an interval? For example, consider f on [0,3] with infinitely many small jumps such that the function still takes every value in some interval? If there are infinitely many discontinuities, the function would take each value in the range, but could it still be an interval? Possibly yes, if the jumps accumulate in such a way that the image covers the whole interval. But if f is strictly increasing and discontinuous at infinitely many points, the image might be an interval if the jumps are arranged such that the gaps are filled by the values that are not attained? Wait: If there are infinitely many discontinuities, say at points x1 < x2 < x3 < ..., each with a positive jump. Then the image of the intervals between discontinuities might be intervals that do not include the jump values. For f to be an interval, every value between the minimum and maximum must be attained. If there are infinitely many discontinuities, then there are infinitely many excluded values (the jump intervals). But the range of a strictly increasing function is always a set that either jumps over some numbers or fills gaps? Actually, if f is a strictly increasing function, its image is a set that is order-isomorphic to the domain (since strictly increasing means injective). But the image might not be an interval if there are gaps. For an arbitrary strictly increasing function, the image is not necessarily an interval; it can be a set that is a union of intervals with gaps. For f(S) to be an interval, we need that there are no gaps. For a strictly increasing function with infinitely many discontinuities, it is possible that the image is still an interval if the jumps are such that the excluded values are covered by other jumps? Actually, the image is determined by the values at every point. If there are infinitely many discontinuities, the function skips certain values at each discontinuity. However, if at some other discontinuity, the function takes exactly those skipped values? That would require the function to take the same value at two different points, which is not allowed because it is strictly increasing (so injective). So each discontinuity creates a skip: the function jumps from a left limit to a right limit, and the values between these limits are not attained. So the image misses an interval. If there are infinitely many such jumps, the image is the union of many intervals with missing gaps between them. To be an interval, all gaps must be filled, but they cannot be filled because the function is strictly increasing and injective: if it skips a value, that value is not attained anywhere. So for the image to be an interval, there must be no gaps. Therefore, the image is an interval if and only if f is continuous. More formally: If f is strictly increasing and f is not continuous anywhere, then its image has gaps at every discontinuity. Actually, if f has a discontinuity at x0, then there exists ε > 0 such that for some δ>0, for all x in (x0-δ, x0+δ), |f(x) - f(x0)| < ε? Actually, for a discontinuity, there is a gap: the set of values between f(x0-) and f(x0+) is not attained. That gap is open because f is strictly increasing. So the image f(S) contains a gap in that open interval. Therefore, if f has any discontinuity, f(S) is not connected. In fact, if f has even one discontinuity, f(S) is disconnected. Because the image of that discontinuity splits the range into two intervals separated by a gap. So the condition that f(S) is connected forces f to be continuous. But wait: Consider the case where the discontinuity is at a boundary where the two intervals join? But there is no x in S that connects the two intervals; the domain S is not connected. So f could have discontinuities at each interval, but if f is continuous on each, then the image is the union of two intervals with a gap if the intervals are separated. That gap would exist if lim_{x->1^-} f(x) < f(2). So to be connected, we need lim_{x->1^-} f(x) = f(2), and also continuity on each part. So they must be continuous as well. So indeed, f must be continuous at every point in S.\n\nBut careful: A function defined on S = [0,1) ∪ [2,3] could have discontinuities only at the endpoints of the intervals? For example, consider f such that on [0,1) it is continuous and on [2,3] it is continuous, but maybe the limit as x approaches 1 from the left is not equal to f(2)? Then there is a gap between the two images, so f(S) is disconnected. But if f is continuous and the images join appropriately, it is connected.\n\nMaybe there is a subtlety: Could f have exactly one discontinuity at a point where the jump is exactly the difference between the supremum of f([0,1)) and the infimum of f([2,3))? That would require a discontinuity at some point in [0,1) or [2,3). As argued, a discontinuity in [0,1) forces a gap below f(2), so even if the jump fills exactly the gap? But the jump in [0,1) is f(x0+) - f(x0-), and f(x0+) < f(2). So the gap from f(x0-) to f(x0+) is below f(2). For the overall image to be connected, we need that there is no gap. So if there is a discontinuity in [0,1), there is definitely a gap between f(x0-) and f(x0+) that is not attained. That gap exists regardless of other discontinuities. So f(S) is disconnected. Similarly, a discontinuity in [2,3] creates a gap above f(2). So any discontinuity within either interval will cause a gap in the image relative to the other interval because the values in the gap are not attained. Therefore, f must have no discontinuities within [0,1) and no discontinuities within [2,3]. So f is continuous on these two intervals.\n\nBut is it possible that f has a discontinuity at a point that is not in S? That is not possible; domain is S. So discontinuities can only occur in S.\n\nThus, f must be continuous on S. So answer is: f is continuous.\n\nHowever, let\'s consider the possibility of a discontinuity at the "gap" point? There is no point in S between 1 and 2, so no discontinuity there.\n\nCould f have infinitely many discontinuities? If it does, then there are infinitely many skipped intervals, so f(S) is disconnected.\n\nSo only option: f is continuous.\n\nBut let\'s test with an example: Suppose f is defined by f(0)=0, f(x)=x on [0,1), and f(x)=2 on [2,3]. Then f is strictly increasing? Check: For x in [0,1), f(x)=x, so increasing. For x in [2,3], f(x)=2 constant? Then f is not strictly increasing because for x > 2, f(x)=2, but for x in [2,3], it is constant, so if x1 < x2 both in [2,3], f(x1)=f(x2)=2, so not strictly increasing. So that\'s not allowed. To be strictly increasing, we need f on [2,3] to also be increasing. So let f(x)=x on [0,1) and f(x)=x on [2,3]. But then the image is [0,1) ∪ [2,3)? Actually, for x in [0,1), f(x) in (0,1); for x in [2,3], f(x) in [2,3]. So f(S) = (0,1) ∪ [2,3] (since f(0)=0, but 0 is attained? f(0)=0, so 0 is in the image). But (0,1) and [2,3] are separated by a gap because 1 > 2. So not connected. To make it connected, we need f(1-) = f(2). So let f(x)=x on [0,1) and f(x)=x-1 on [2,3]? Then f(x) on [2,3] gives: at x=2, f(2)=1; at x=3, f(3)=2. So f(S) = [0,1) ∪ [1,2] = [0,2] (since 1 is attained at x=2). But check strict increasing: For x in [0,1), f(x)=x; for x in [2,3], f(x)=x-1. At the boundary: For x=1 (not in S) and x=2: f(1-) = 1, f(2)=1, so f(1-) = f(2). So the function is increasing overall? But is it strictly increasing? For x1=0.9, x2=2, f(0.9)=0.9, f(2)=1, so 0.9 < 1, okay. For x1=1.9? Not in domain because 1.9 is in [0,1)? Actually 1.9 is not in [0,1); it\'s greater than 1. So we only have points in [0,1) and [2,3]. So for any x1 in [0,1) and x2 in [2,3], x1 < 1 < 2, so x1 < x2, so f(x1) < f(x1) (since f(x1) < 1 and f(x2) ≥ 1), so f(x1) < f(x2). So it is strictly increasing. And it is continuous? Check continuity at the "gap": There is no x in S that connects, so we don\'t have a discontinuity at any point in S. Actually, is f continuous on [0,1)? Yes, f(x)=x is continuous. On [2,3], f(x)=x-1 is continuous. So f is continuous on S. And f(S)=[0,2] is connected. So this example works with no discontinuities.\n\nNow consider a function that has a discontinuity at some point in [0,1): f(x)=x for x in [0,1/2) and f(x)=x+0.5 for x in [1/2,1), and on [2,3], f(x)=x. Then f is strictly increasing? For x in [0,1/2), f(x)=x; for x in [1/2,1), f(x)=x+0.5. So at x=1/2, f(1/2)=0.5+0.5=1, but for x<1/2, f(x)<0.5, so f is increasing. For x in [2,3], f(x)=x, so increasing. Overall, for any x1 in [0,1) and x2 in [2,3], f(x1) < 1 and f(x2) ≥ 2, so f(x1) < f(x2). So strictly increasing. But is it continuous? At x=1/2, f is discontinuous because left limit 0.5, right limit 1. So f has exactly one discontinuity in [0,1). Then f(S) consists of pieces: On [0,1/2), image (0,0.5) (since f(0)=0, but 0 is attained? Actually f(0)=0, so 0 is in the image). On [1/2,1), image (1,1.5) (since f(1/2)=1, and for x>1/2, f(x)>1). On [2,3], image [2,3]. So f(S) = [0,0.5) ∪ [1,1.5) ∪ [2,3]. This is clearly disconnected: there is a gap between 0.5 and 1, and between 1.5 and 2. So f(S) is not connected. So having a discontinuity leads to disconnected f(S).\n\nConsider a function with a discontinuity in [2,3]: f(x)=x on [