3+f5)-(δ+ε)), we can compute for N=75: f3 = fractional part of 75/3 = 0, f5 = fractional part of 75/5 = 0, so f3+f5=0. δ=0, ε=0. Then ((f3+f5)-(δ+ε)) = 0, so k = 7+15*0=7, but actual k=37, so contradiction. So N=75 does not satisfy.\n\nSo the condition k = 7+15((f3+f5)-(δ+ε)) is necessary and sufficient. For k=37, we need (f3+f5)-(δ+ε) = (37-7)/15 = 30/15=2. That means (f3+f5)-(δ+ε)=2. Since f3+f5 <2, and δ+ε ≥0, this requires δ+ε=0 and f3+f5=2, but f3+f5 cannot be 2 because both fractional parts are less than 1. So impossible. So k cannot be 37. The maximum possible k is when (f3+f5)-(δ+ε) is as large as possible. The maximum value of f3+f5 is 2 (if both fractional parts are 1, but that\'s impossible since fractional part is strictly less than 1). Actually maximum is less than 2. And δ+ε can be 0,1,2. So the maximum of (f3+f5)-(δ+ε) is less than 2. So k < 7+30=37. So k ≤ 36? Actually if f3+f5 can be arbitrarily close to 2, say 1.999, and δ+ε=0, then (f3+f5)-(δ+ε) can be close to 2, so k can be close to 37 but not equal to 37. But k is integer, so maximum possible k is when (f3+f5)-(δ+ε) = 2 - something. But since f3+f5 <2, the maximum possible value of (f3+f5)-(δ+ε) is strictly less than 2, so k can be at most floor(7+2*15) = floor(37) = 36? Actually if (f3+f5)-(δ+ε) can be 1.999, then k=7+15*1.999=7+29.985=36.985, so k can be 36. But we need exact equality. So we need (f3+f5)-(δ+ε) to be exactly (k-7)/15. Since (f3+f5) and (δ+ε) are specific rational numbers depending on k, it\'s a specific condition. So odd N are bounded, and likely the largest N is 37. We found odd N=37 works. Is there odd N >37 that works? Let\'s try N=39: k=19, (k-7)/15 = 12/15=0.8. Need (f3+f5)-(δ+ε)=0.8. For N=39: f3 = fractional part of 39/3 = 0, f5 = fractional part of 39/5 = 0.8, so f3+f5=0.8. δ: fractional part of 39/3=0, so δ=0; ε: fractional part of 39/5=0.8>0.5, so ε=1. So (δ+ε)=1. Then (f3+f5)-(δ+ε)=0.8-1= -0.2, not 0.8. So doesn\'t work.\nN=41: k=20, (20-7)/15=13/15≈0.8667. Compute 