t 0.3333; f5=61/5=12.2, fractional part 0.2; f3+f5=0.5333. δ: δ=0, ε=0; δ+ε=0. So (f3+f5)-(δ+ε)=0.5333.\nN=63: k=31, (31-7)/15=24/15=1.6. f3=63/3=21 exactly, fractional part 0; f5=63/5=12.6, fractional part 0.6; f3+f5=0.6. δ:0, ε: 0.6>0.5, ε=1; δ+ε=1. So (f3+f5)-(δ+ε)= -0.4.\nN=65: k=32, (32-7)/15=25/15≈1.6667. f3=65/3=21.6667, fractional part 0.6667; f5=65/5=13 exactly, fractional part 0; f3+f5=0.6667. δ: f3>0.5, δ=1; ε: 0, ε=0; δ+ε=1. So (f3+f5)-(δ+ε)= -0.3333.\nN=67: k=33, (33-7)/15=26/15≈1.7333. f3=67/3=22.3333, fractional part 0.3333; f5=67/5=13.4, fractional part 0.4; f3+f5=0.7333. δ: f3≤0.5, δ=0; ε: f5≤0.5, ε=0; δ+ε=0. So (f3+f5)-(δ+ε)=0.7333.\nN=69: k=34, (34-7)/15=27/15=1.8. f3=69/3=23 exactly, fractional part 0; f5=69/5=13.8, fractional part 0.8; f3+f5=0.8. δ:0, ε: f5>0.5, ε=1; δ+ε=1. So (f3+f5)-(δ+ε)= -0.2.\nN=71: k=35, (35-7)/15=28/15≈1.8667. f3=71/3=23.6667, fractional part 0.6667; f5=71/5=14.2, fractional part 0.2; f3+f5=0.8667. δ: f3>0.5, δ=1; ε: f5≤0.5, ε=0; δ+ε=1. So (f3+f5)-(δ+ε)= -0.1333.\nN=73: k=36, (36-7)/15=29/15≈1.9333. f3=73/3=24.3333, fractional part 0.3333; f5=73/5=14.6, fractional part 0.6; f3+f5=0.9333. δ: δ=0, ε: ε=1; δ+ε=1. So (f3+f5)-(δ+ε)= -0.0667.\nN=75: we computed: f3+f5=0, δ+ε=0, difference=0.\nSo none of these give exactly the required (k-7)/15. For k=18 (N=37), we had required (18-7)/15=11/15≈0.7333. For N=37: f3 = fractional part of 37/3 = 1/3 ≈0.3333; f5 = fractional part of 37/5 = 2/5=0.4; f3+f5=0.7333; δ: f3≤0.5, δ=0; ε: f5≤0.5? 0.4≤0.5, so ε=0; δ+ε=0. So (f3+f5)-(δ+ε)=0.7333 exactly equals 11/15? 11/15=0.73333..., yes exactly. So that works.\n\nWe haven\'t found any odd N >37 that works. So possibly the largest odd N is 37.\n\nNow for even N, we have condition: k = 15(f3+f5) - 15(δ+ε), where f3,f5 are fractional parts of 2k/3 and 2k/5. We can try to find larger even N by searching numerically. We already searched up to 100 and found even N: 4,6,10,12,16,22. Let\'s extend the search a bit further, maybe up to 200 or 300.\n\nWe can write a small program in mind, but since I\'m reasoning manually, I\'ll try to find pattern.\n\nWe want even N=2k such that round(N/3)+round(N/5)=k, i.e., round(2k/3)+round(2k/5)=k. Let\'s search k.\n\nFor k=8 (N=16): round(16/3)=5, round(16/5)=3, sum=8, works. So k=8 works.\nFor k=11 (N=22): round(22/3)=7, round(22/5)=4, sum=11, works.\nFor k=16 (N=32): round(32/3)=11, round(32/5)=6, sum=17 ≠16. So fails.\nFor k=18 (N=36): round(36/3)=12, round(36/5)=8, sum=20 ≠18. Fails.\nFor k=23 (N=46): round(46/3)=15, round(46/5)=9, sum=24 ≠23. Fails.\nFor k=28 (N=56): round(56/3)=19, round(56/5)=11, sum=30 ≠28. Fails.\nFor k=30 (N=60): round(60/3)=20, round(60/5)=12, sum=32 ≠30. Fails.\nFor k=34 (N=68): round(68/3)=23, round(68/5)=14, sum=37 ≠34. Fails.\nFor k=38 (N=76): round(76/3)=25, round(76/5)=15, sum=40 ≠38. Fails.\nFor k=40 (N=80): round(80/3)=27, round(80/5)=16, sum=43 ≠40. Fails.\nFor k=44 (N=88): round(88/3)=29, round(88/5)=17, sum=46 ≠44. Fails.\nFor k=48 (N=96): round(96/3)=32, round(96/5)=19, sum=51 ≠48. Fails.\nFor k=50 (N=100): round(100/3)=33, round(100/5)=20, sum=53 ≠50. Fails.\nFor k=55 (N=110): round(110/3)=36, round(110/5)=22, sum=58 ≠55. Fails.\nFor k=60 (N=120): round(120/3)=40, round(120/5)=24, sum=64 ≠60. Fails.\nFor k=65 (N=130): round(130/3)=43, round(130/5)=26, sum=69 ≠65. Fails.\nFor k=70 (N=140): round(140/3)=46, round(140/5)=28, sum=74 ≠70. Fails.\nFor k=73 (N=146): round(146/3)=49, round(146/5)=29, sum=78 ≠73. Fails.\nFor k=78 (N=156): round(156/3)=52, round(156/5)=31, sum=83 ≠78. Fails.\nFor k=80 (N=160): round(160/3)=53, round(160/5)=32, sum=85 ≠80. Fails.\nFor k=85 (N=170): round(170/3)=56, round(170/5)=34, sum=90 ≠85. Fails.\nFor k=90 (N=180): round(180/3)=60, round(180/5)=36, sum=96 ≠90. Fails.\nFor k=95 (N=190): round(190/3)=63, round(190/5)=38, sum=101 ≠95. Fails.\nFor k=98 (N=196): round(196/3)=65, round(196/5)=39, sum=104 ≠98. Fails.\nFor k=99 (N=198): round(198/3)=66, round(198/5)=40, sum=106 ≠99. Fails.\n\nSo even N up to 200 seem to be only those we found earlier. But maybe there is an even N larger than 100 that works. Let\'s check k=101 (N=202): round(202/3)=67 (202/3=67.333), round(202/5)=40 (202/5=40.4), sum=107, not 101.\nk=102 (N=204): round(204/3)=68, round(204/5)=41 (204/5=40.8), sum=109.\nk=103 (N=206): round(206/3)=68 (206/3=68.666), round(206/5)=41 (206/5=41.2), sum=109.\nk=104 (N=208): round(208/3)=69 (208/3=69.333), round(208/5)=41 (208/5=41.6), sum=110.\nk=105 (N=210): round(210/3)=70, round(210/5)=42, sum=112.\nk=106 (N=212): round(212/3)=71 (212/3=70.666? Actually 212/3=70.666? Wait 212/3=70.666? 3*70=210, so 212/3=70.666..., round to nearest integer: distance to 70 is 0.666, to 71 is 0.333, so nearest 71. round(212/5)=42 (212/5=42.4), sum=113.\nk=107 (N=214): round(214/3)=71 (214/3=71.333), round(214/5)=43 (214/5=42.8), sum=114.\nk=108 (N=216): round(216/3)=72, round(216/5)=43 (216/5=43.2), sum=115.\nk=109 (N=218): round(218/3)=73 (218/3=72.666), round(218/5)=44 (218/5=43.6), sum=117.\nk=110 (N=220): round(220/3)=73 (220/3=73.333), round(220/5)=44, sum=117.\n\nNone of these seem to give sum equal to k. The sum seems to be always larger than k. For even N, we have L = k, so condition is R+F = k. But R ≈ 2k/3, F ≈ 2k/5, sum ≈ (8/15)k. To have R+F = k, we need rounding to increase R and F significantly. But rounding error is at most 1 per number, so maximum sum increase is 2. So R+F ≤ (2k/3 + 1) + (2k/5 + 1) = 2k/3+2k/5+2 = (16k/15)+2. For R+F = k, we need (16k/15)+2 ≥ k, i.e., k/15 + 2 ≥ 0, which is true for all k. But we also need R+F exactly equal to k, so we need rounding errors such that the total rounding up is exactly k - (2k/3+2k/5) = k - (16k/15) = (-k/15) + (15k/15) - (16k/15) = (-k/15)? Actually k - (16k/15) = (15k/15 - 16k/15) = -k/15. That is negative for positive k. So we need rounding up to be positive and more than that negative part. Actually, if rounding tends to be down, then we need rounding up to be large. So we need δ+ε to compensate for the fact that a+b is typically less than k? Wait, for even N, a+b = floor(2k/3)+floor(2k/5). That sum is approximately 16k/15, but since floors are integers, they might be less than the exact fractions. In some cases, a+b could be less than 16k/15, possibly even less than k. For example, for k=8 (N=16), a+b = floor(16/3)=5 + floor(16/5)=3 =8, exactly k. And rounding errors: round(16/3)=5 (since 16/3=5.333, rounding to nearest integer gives 5, no error), round(16/5)=3 (16/5=3.2, rounding to 3, no error). So sum a+b=8, and δ+ε=0, so sum=8=k. So that works. For k=11 (N=22), a+b = floor(22/3)=7 + floor(22/5)=4 =11, exactly k, and rounding errors: round(22/3)=7 (22/3=7.333, rounds to 7, no error), round(22/5)=4 (22/5=4.4, rounds to 4, no error). So sum=11=k. For k=16 (N=32), a+b = floor(32/3)=10+floor(32/5)=6=16, exactly k? Actually 10+6=16, exactly k. But rounding errors: round(32/3)=11 (32/3=10.666, rounds to 11, so δ=1), round(32/5)=6 (32/5=6.4, rounds to 6, ε=0), so sum = a+b+δ+ε = 16+1=17 ≠ k=16. So that fails because rounding increased one number. So the key is that for even N, if round(N/3) and round(N/5) are both exactly the floor values (no rounding up), then their sum equals k provided that floor(N/3)+floor(N/5)=k. But if one of them rounds up, then sum exceeds k. So we need floor(N/3)+floor(N/5)=k, and also rounding does not increase either number. That means both N/3 and N/5 must have fractional parts ≤ 0.5, so that rounding is to the floor. For N=16: N/3=5.333, fractional part 0.333 ≤0.5, so rounds to 5; N/5=3.2, fractional part 0.2 ≤0.5, so rounds to 3. For N=22: N/3=7.333, frac 0.333 ≤0.5; N/5=4.4, frac 0.4 ≤0.5. So both fractional parts ≤0.5. For N=4: N/3=1.333, frac 0.333; N/5=0.8, frac 0.8 >0.5, so rounding for N/5 is to ceil, so round to 2? Wait, careful: For N=4, round(4/5)=1, not 2. Because 4/5=0.8, the nearest integer is 1 (distance 0.2) vs 2 (distance 1.2), so round to 1. That is floor, not ceil. So rounding rule: round(x) = floor(x+0.5). For x=0.8, x+0.5=1.3, f