om a set of 5 cousins to a set of 4 rooms, modulo permutation of the rooms. That is the number of orbits of these functions under the action of the symmetric group on the rooms. That number is given by Burnside\'s lemma. But the sum of Stirling numbers is simpler.\n\nBut careful: If rooms are identical, does that mean we also consider the arrangement within each room? The problem says "arranged among the rooms". Possibly that means the cousins are assigned to rooms, and within a room they might be arranged? But no, it says "cousins be arranged among the rooms", likely meaning they are assigned to rooms. Possibly they are just placed into rooms, so no arrangement within rooms. So just the assignment.\n\nThus answer should be 51.\n\nWait: Could there be an alternative interpretation: "arranged" might imply that we also consider the order of cousins in each room? But typically, when assigning people to rooms, we don\'t care about the order within the room. The phrase "arranged among the rooms" might simply mean distributed among rooms. Usually combinatorial problems like this: "How many ways can n children be divided into k identical groups?" That is Stirling numbers. So I\'ll go with 51.\n\nBut let\'s double-check with Burnside\'s lemma for verification.\n\nWe have 4 rooms considered indistinguishable. So we have 4! possible labeled assignments (if rooms were labeled and each assignment is a function from cousins to rooms). Burnside\'s lemma says the number of orbits (i.e., distributions up to room permutation) is (1/4!) * sum_{permutations of rooms} number of assignments fixed by that permutation. But simpler: The number of assignments with indistinguishable rooms is equal to the number of partitions into at most 4 parts. So it\'s 51.\n\nAnother approach: Count the number of ways to distribute 5 distinct objects into 4 indistinguishable boxes. That is the same as the number of set partitions with at most 4 blocks. So indeed.\n\nWe compute S(5,1)=1, S(5,2)=15, S(5,3)=