al = value after investing X+100 for 30 days. So difference: Credit - Debit = (X+100) - (X-20,000 + interest on (X-20,000)) = 100 + 20,000 - interest on (X-20,000) = 120,000 - interest on (X-20,000). For credit to be better, we need 120,000 > interest on (X-20,000). That depends on X, and if X is large, debit is better. So again, condition on X.\n\nUnless we assume X is the same, but we are comparing the two choices starting from the same initial amount of cash not used for the purchase. That seems standard.\n\nBut the problem does not specify X. So maybe we assume that we have exactly 20,000 cash readily available? That is, if we have 20,000, paying with debit means we use all cash, paying with credit means we keep all cash and borrow 20,000. Then X=20,000. In that case, debit: we spend all our cash, so we have no cash to invest. But interest on 0 is 0. Credit: we have 20,000 cash and borrow 20,000, we get 100 cashback. So after 1 month, credit: we have 20,000 cash (the borrowed one is reduced by repayment? Actually, if we have borrow 20,000 and repay anyway, it\'s like we overpaid. But we borrow 20,000, so our net worth is 20,000 cash + 20,000 borrowed. After 1 month, we disagree the loan? Possibly we now have 20,000 cash and 20,000 loan, and then we get cashback of 100, so we have 20,000 cash and 20,000 loan, which could be invested. But the loan repayment is within N days, but at month 1, we might have already repaid? Actually, we pay within N days, so we repay by month N. At month 1, if N>=1, we have repaid. So at month 1, we have 20,000 cash plus the 20,000 money we borrowed, but we paid it back, so effectively we have 40,000 cash, plus cashback 100, total 40,100. But careful: When we borrow 20,000, we owe 20,000. But we also have the initial 20,000 cash. If we then make the purchase with credit card, we use cash to authorize, so we owe 20,000 and have 20,000 cash initially. After the purchase, we have 20,000 cash and 20,000 debt. When we repay within N days, 