t card."\n\nKey phrases: "under which all other conditions being equal", "more profitable". So we compare two scenarios: paying with debit card, and paying with credit card but with some specific N. N is part of the credit card condition: you must repay within N days to stay within the grace period. So the credit card scenario includes a condition: repay within N days. That means if we pay with credit card, we are constrained to repay within N days. So if we pay within that period, we avoid extra charges. If we pay outside that period, maybe we incur extra charges (interest). So the credit card scenario we are comparing includes the constraint of NOT having extra charges, i.e., we are buying using the credit card from the credit limit system, but we are forced to repay within N days (maybe it\'s the only allowable period). So we compare debit card payment (immediate, no penalty) vs credit card payment made within N days (no penalty from credit). The cashback is after 1 month regardless. So as argued, N seems irrelevant for the comparison because cashback is received at the end of month 1, and no extra charges are incurred up to N days. So the profit from paying within N days is the same as paying immediately (since paying immediately is within N days for any N>0). But paying immediately might avoid potential penalties if N is small? If we pay immediately, we don\'t have to pay the debit card amount, so no penalty. If we pay with credit card: we must repay within N days. If we repay within N days, we avoid extra charges, and we still get cashback. So if we repay within N days, the situation is exactly the same as if we had repaid immediately. So the only difference is the timing of cashback: cashback is after 1 month regardless, so no difference. So the profit is exactly the same as if we had repaid immediately. So it\'s always more profitable to use credit card if N is at least 1 day, because paying immediately is a subset of paying within N days. So N would be 1. But that seems too simple.\n\nMaybe there is a twist: If we pay with credit card but not within N days, we would have to pay the full amount plus interest earlier. So if we want to use credit card, we can either pay within N days to avoid penalty, or pay outside to pay full amount, but then we lose cashback? Possibly cashback is only given if payment is made within N days. That would make sense: to get cashback, you must pay within the grace period. So if you pay without the grace period, you don\'t get cashback. Then the two options are: debit card payment (immediate, no cashback, no penalty) vs credit card payment made within N days (no penalty, possible cashback if within N days? But cashback is after 1 month, so if you pay within N days, you still get cashback because cashback is after 1 month, not after N days). So cashback is independent of N. So again, same.\n\nMaybe cashback is given only if you make the first payment within 1 month, regardless of N. So cashback is not time-sensitive with respect to N.\n\nGiven the lack of clarity, I\'ll look for a common type of problem: Often in such problems, they compare the net effect considering interest earned on the average balance and the cashback received. They also consider the grace period for credit card as a constraint: if you pay after N days, you pay immediately and get cashback later? That doesn\'t make sense.\n\nAnother interpretation: When you pay with credit card, you commit to repay within N days. If you pay within that period, you don\'t pay interest, and you get cashback after 1 month. If you pay after N days, you pay immediately (and get cashback later? Or not?) but you would have to pay the full amount at the time of purchase? Not sure.\n\nMaybe the cashback is paid at the time of the first transaction, but with credit card, you can delay the payment, so you get cashback early if you repay early. But it says after 1 month.\n\nI think the most plausible interpretation is that we are comparing over a on