n within the month.\n\nBut maybe we cannot repay within the month because N might be less than 30? If N < 30, we might not have time to repay by month 30, so we still have the loan outstanding in the month. So at the end of the month, with debt still outstanding, the net asset is X+100 (cashback) minus the loan of 20,000? Actually, the loan is a liability, so net worth is X+100 (cash) - 20,000 (loan) = X - 19000. That is worse than debit if X is small. So if N < 30 and we do not repay by month 30, we have debt, which reduces our net worth. So the profit depends on whether we repay the loan within the month. That is, if N is large enough that we can repay by month 30, then credit gives higher net worth because we repay the loan and also get cashback. If N is small, we might not repay by month 30, so we have debt and net worth is lower.\n\nTherefore, the minimum N such that it is more profitable to use credit card is the N such that we can repay the loan within 30 days, i.e., N >= 30. But if N < 30, we cannot repay by month 30, so we have to stop payment and pay the full amount, which might incur extra charges. But the problem condition is that we pay within N days to stay within grace period. So if we want to use credit card and also get cashback, we must repay within N days. If N < 30, we might have to repay before month 1? But cashback is after 1 month, so if we repay before month 1, we might not get cashback? Not necessarily.\n\nThe problem statement: "If you pay for the purchase with the credit card (the credit limit allows it), you will have to repay the bank within N days to stay within the grace period in which the credit can be repaid without extra charges. Additionally, in this case, the bank will pay cashback of 0.5% of the purchase amount after 1 month." This implies that when you pay with credit card, you are subject to a time limit N within which you must repay to avoid extra charges. It also says you will get cashback after 1 month. So it seems you have