e the interest earned.\n\nMaybe the problem implies that when we use debit card, we put the purchase amount into the debit account, so our debit account balance increases by 20,000 temporarily, but then it decreases by 20,000 when we pay, so the net effect on the balance is zero? That seems misleading.\n\nI think a more straightforward approach is to consider the net effect on our wealth at the end of the month, assuming we start with our debit account balance before purchase. But we also have cash on the debit card if we have surplus funds.\n\nMaybe the problem is typical: We have a certain amount of cash. We can either use it immediately (debit) or borrow 20,000 via credit card (within N days) to get cashback. The benefit of credit card is the cashback minus the opportunity cost of not investing the 20,000 for 30 days. The opportunity cost is the interest we would have earned on that 20,000 if we had invested it. So we compare: Cashback - Interest on 20,000. That gives: 100 - 0.15*20,000 = 100 - 3000 = -2900, so credit is worse. But that uses the full 20,000 as invested, while debit only invests X-20,000. So the difference is: Credit: we have X cash and 100 cashback, so we invest X for 30 days. Debit: we invest X-20,000 for 30 days. So the extra 20,000 invested in credit gives an extra 20,0 times the interest rate over 30 days. But also we get 100. So credit net = X + 100 + 30*0.005*(X) (if we invest from month 1). Debit net = (X-20,000) + 30*0.005*(X-20,000). So credit net = X + 100 + 0.15X. Debit net = X - 20,000 + 0.15X - 3 = X + 0.15X - 20,003 = 1.15X - 20,003. So difference = credit - debit = (X+0.15X+100) - (1.15X - 20,003) = 0.15X+100 - 1.15X + 20,003 = -0.15X + 20,103. So credit is better when 20,103 > 0.15X => X < 20,103/0.15 = 134,020 rubles. So for very large X, credit is better; for small X, debit is better. That seems too extreme.\n\nI think the earlier formulation with debit net = 1.15*(X-20,000) and credit net = X+100 is more plausible. That gives credit better when 100 > 0.15*(X-20,000) => X < 20,000 + 100/0.15 = 20,000 + 666.67 = 20,666.67. So if initial cash X is less than 20,666.67, credit is better. That is a realistic threshold: if you have less than about 20,666.67 rubles, using credit card gives you a net advantage because you get 100 rubles cashback and you have the 20,000 cash back, while debit costs you interest on whatever amount exceeds 20,000. But this still depends on X.\n\nMaybe the problem assumes that we have exactly 20,000 rubles in our cash. Then debit: spend all 20,000, so X=20,000. Then debit net: 1.15*(0) = 0. Credit net: X+100 = 20,100. So credit is better. So for X=20,000, credit is always better regardless of N. So N can be any positive number. That seems too trivial.\n\nMaybe the problem expects that we consider the cashback as a benefit offsetting the interest we would have earned if we had invested the 20,000. And we need to find N such that the benefit of cashback exceeds the interest on the 20,000 for the same time period as cashback? But cashback is after 1 month, so the 20,000 is invested for 29 days after cashback? Or maybe the interest is also over 30 days? Possibly the interest on the debit account is credited at the end of the month, so we earn interest on the average balance over the month. If we invest the 20,000 from the start of the month, we earn interest over 30 days. So the benefit of cashback is 100 rubles over 30 days, or equivalent to a 6.67% monthly rate. But the interest on 20,000 for 30 days is 3000 rubles. So cashback is five times less expensive.\n\nGiven these inconsistencies, I suspect the intended interpretation is simpler: Compare the net effect after one month, assuming that with debit card we pay the 20,000 immediately, so our debit account balance decreases by 20,000, and we earn interest on the reduced balance. With credit card we pay within N days, but we also get 0.5% cashback after 1 month. The key is that with credit card, we keep the 20,000 as ca