n outstanding. So debit balance + cashback but also paying off the loan reduces our wealth. But the loan is just a debit entry, so our debit balance remains unchanged. The loan is a debit entry (outgoing), so it reduces our balance by 20,000. So at month 1, if N > 30, our net balance = B0 - 20,000 (debit) + 100 (cashback) - 20,000 (loan repayment) = B0 + 100 - 20,000 = B0 - 19,900.\nIf N <= 30, we repay the loan within the month, so at month 1, the loan repayment is already made, so net balance = B0 + 100.\n\nSo we have:\nIf N <= 30: Net = B0 + 100.\nIf N > 30: Net = B0 - 19,900.\n\nFor debit card: Net = 1.15*(B0 - 20,000).\n\nTo compare, we need to know B0. The problem does not specify B0. Possibly B0 is the amount we have in our debit account before the purchase. If we have exactly 20,000, then B0 = 20,000. Then debit Net = 1.15*(0) = 0. Credit Net = B0 + 100 = 20,100 if N <= 30. So credit is better. If N > 30, credit Net = 20,000 - 19,900 = 100. So still credit is better. So for B0=20,000, credit is always better.\n\nIf we have more than 20,000, say B0 > 20,000, then we have surplus. For B0 = 25,000, debit Net = 1.15*(5,000) = 5,750. Credit Net = B0 + 100 = 25,100 if N <= 30. So credit is better. For N > 30, credit Net = B0 - 19,900 = 5,100. Still better. So for B0=25,000, credit is better.\nFor B0 = 30,000, debit Net = 1.15*(10,000) = 11,500. Credit Net = 30,100 if N<=30, so better. Credit Net = 30,000 - 19,900 = 10,100 if N>30, still better.\nFor B0 = 40,000, debit Net = 1.15*(20,000) = 23,000. Credit Net = 40,100 if N<=30, better. Credit Net = 40,000 - 19,900 = 20,100 if N>30. Now debit Net > credit Net if N>30: 23,000 > 20,100? Actually debit Net = 23,000, credit Net = 20,100, so debit is better. So for B0=40,000, if N > 30, debit is better. If N <= 30, credit is much better.\n\nSo the transition happens at some B0 where debit Net becomes greater than credit Net for N>30. That is when 1.15*(B0 - 20,000) = B0 - 19,900. Solve: 1.15B0 - 23,000 = B0 - 19,900 => 1.15B0 - B0 = 23,000 - 19,900 => 0.15B0 = 3,100 => B0 = 3,100/0.15 = 20,666.67. So for B0 < 20,666.67, debit Net > credit Net for N>30. For B0 > 20,666.67, debit Net < credit Net for N>30. And for N<=30, credit Net is always greater than debit Net because B0 + 100 > 1.15*(B0-20,000) for B0 >= 0? Check: B0+100 > 1.15B0 - 23,000 => 0 > 0.15B0 - 23,100 => 0.15B0 < 23,100 => B0 < 23,100/0.15 = 155,000. So for B0 < 155,000, credit is better when N<=30. For B0 > 155,000, debit is better even with N<=30. So the threshold on B0 is 155,000 rubles. That is huge. If we have that much money, debit is better. If we have less, credit is better. But we are buying a 20,000 ticket, so presumably we have around that amount, maybe less. But the problem doesn\'t specify B0, so maybe we assume B0 = 0? That is unrealistic because we need to pay 20,000, so we need at least 20,000 in our account to pay with debit. So B0 >= 20,000. If B0 = 20,000, credit is better. If B0 is slightly above 20,666.67, debit is better. But there is no clear answer.\n\nGiven the problem asks for N, and N appears in the condition for loan repayment time, I think the intended answer is that N must be at most 30 to avoid having the loan outstanding at month 1, so that we can utilize the 20,000 cash from the loan. But as we saw, even if N > 30, credit might still be better if B0 is small. But the problem likely expects a specific N independent of B0. Maybe we assume that B0 is exactly the amount needed for the purchase, i.e., B0 = 20,000. Then credit is always better. But that would not require solving for N.\n\nMaybe the problem is asking for the minimum N such that the cashback is received before the end of the month? That would be N = 1? But cashback is after 1 month, not immediately.\n\nAnother idea: The cashback is paid after 1 month, but if we pay with credit card within N days, we might get the cashback immediately? That doesn\'t make sense.\n\nGiven the confusion, I\'ll check online memory: There is a known 