ntity or they move 1 to an even position? But 2 and 4 are even? Actually 2 and 4 are even indices? Not sure.\n\nMaybe we can list all possible outcomes from applying the operations repeatedly to the initial tuple (1,2,3,4). Instead of thinking in terms of permutations, we can enumerate states by applying operations repeatedly to the tuple. Since the group might be small, we can try to compute the orbit by starting from (1,2,3,4) and applying all operations repeatedly to see how many distinct states we can get.\n\nWe\'ll denote state as a tuple (R1,R2,R3,R4). Initially: (1,2,3,4).\n\nOperation 1: swaps R1 and R3, swaps R2 and R4: so after op1, state becomes (3,4,1,2).\n\nOperation 2: transfers contents: R2→R3, R3→R4, R4→R2: so from initial (1,2,3,4) becomes (1,4,2,3). From (3,4,1,2), op2 gives: R2=4 goes to R3, R3=1 goes to R4, R4=2 goes to R2. So new state: (3,2,4,1). From (1,2,3,4), op2 gives (1,4,2,3). From (3,4,1,2), op2 gives (3,2,4,1). From (1,4,2,3), op2 gives: R2=4→R3, R3=2→R4, R4=3→R2, so (1,3,4,2). From (3,2,4,1), op2 gives: R2=2→R3, R3=4→R4, R4=1→R2, so (3,1,2,4). From (3,4,1,2) after op2 gave (3,2,4,1). From etc.\n\nWe can continue generating states. Let\'s list systematically. We can think of these operations as linear transformations on a 4-dimensional vector (values in {1,2,3,4}). They generate a group acting on the tuple. The number of possible outcomes is the size of the orbit of the initial tuple under the group action. Since the operations are invertible, the orbit is a subset of the set of all permutations of {1,2,3,4} (i.e., all possible assignments of digits 1,2,3,4 to registers). There are 4! = 24 possible tuples overall. We want to count how many of these can be obtained.\n\nLet\'s generate states systematically by applying both operations. We\'ll consider applying both operations in sequences. Since the operations are invertible, we can explore until we cycle back.\n\nDefine T1 and T2 as functions:\nT1(a,b,c,d) = (c,d,a,b)\nT2(a,b,c,d) = (a,d,b,c)\n\nWe start from I = (1,2,3,4).\n\nApply T1 to I: S1 = (3,4,1,2).\nApply T2 to I: S2 = (1,4,2,3).\n\nFrom S1:\nT1(S1) = (1,2,3,4) = I.\nT2(S1) = (3,2,4,1).\n\nFrom S2:\nT1(S2) = (2,3,4,1)?\nT2(S2) = (1,3,4,2).\n\nFrom (3,2,4,1):\nT1 = (4,1,3,2)\nT2 = (3,1,2,4)\n\nFrom (1,3,4,2):\nT1 = (4,2,1,3)\nT2 = (1,2,4,3)\n\nFrom (3,1,2,4):\nT1 = (2,4,3,1)\nT2 = (3,4,1,2)? Actually T2: (R1,R2,R3,R4) = (3,1,2,4) becomes (3,4,1,2) which is S1.\n\nFrom (4,1,3,2):\nT1 = (3,2,4,1) which is already.\nT2 = (4,2,1,3)\n\nFrom (4,2,1,3):\nT1 = (1,3,4,2) which is already.\nT2 = (4,3,2,1)\n\nFrom (2,4,3,1):\nT1 = (3,1,2,4) which is already.\nT2 = (2,1,4,3)\n\nFrom (1,2,4,3):\nT1 = (4,3,1,2)\nT2 = (1,3,2,4)\n\nFrom (2,1,4,3):\nT1 = (4,3,2,1)\nT2 = (2,3,1,4)\n\nFrom (4,3,2,1):\nT1 = (2,1,4,3) already.\nT2 = (4,1,3,2) already.\n\nFrom (1,3,2,4):\nT1 = (2,4,1,3)\nT2 = (1,4,2,3) which is S2? Actually S2 was (1,4,2,3). So indeed T2(1,3,2,4) = (1,4,2,3).\n\nFrom (2,3,1,4):\nT1 = (1,4,2,3) which is S2.\nT2 = (2,4,3,1) which is already.\n\nFrom (1,4,2,3):\nWe already visited.\n\nFrom (3,4,1,2):\nWe already visited.\n\nNow we have new states: (4,3,2,1), (2,4,1,3), (2,1,4,3), (2,3,1,4), (1,4,1,3)? Actually (1,4,1,3) no, we had (1,3,4,2), (4,3,1,2), etc.\n\nLet\'s list all distinct tuples we have encountered so far:\n\n1. (1,2,3,4)\n2. (3,4,1,2)\n3. (1,4,2,3)\n4. (3,2,4,1)\n5. (1,3,4,2)\n6. (3,1,2,4)\n7. (4,1,3,2)\n8. (4,2,1,3)\n9. (3,1,2,4) already.\n10. (4,3,2,1)\n11. (1,3,2,4)\n12. (2,1,4,3)\n13. (2,4,3,1)\n14. (1,2,4,3)\n15. (4,3,1,2)\n16. (1,4,2,3) already.\n17. (2,3,1,4)\n\nAlso we had (4,2,1,3) and (4,3,2,1) and (2,4,1,3)? We missed (2,4,1,3) maybe from (4,3,2,1) T1 gave (4,2,1,3) not (2,4,1,3). (2,4,1,3) appears from (4,2,1,3) T1? Actually (4,2,1,3) T1 = (1,3,4,2) not (2,4,1,3). So we need to check if we have (2,4,1,3). From (1,3,4,2) T1 = (4,2,1,3) not (2,4,1,3). From (2,1,4,3) T1 = (4,3,2,1). From (1,4,2,3) T1 = (2,3,4,1) not in list. So (2,4,1,3) not yet? Wait, we had state (2,1,4,3) 